e $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/e0/e07fca985a11d4edb66ea91d9a560ab134d81f5f20aaba8b376677d0de2dd3e7.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/78/78de6ce0133bec33c14eabb43db2e53eb8d76653aa1fc121c83f47c4b91c3b94.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "831cb1b1-064e-559a-8090-c5ef5904d2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463482} {:answer/id #uuid "f119cf42-93c5-5b60-9a16-92306e80bd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463482} :answer-field/id #uuid "069a4e64-f502-58b7-afe8-73851d7f80b9" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464675} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59c28f9-7a4d-56a4-8b36-20c269b2a473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463484} {:answer/id #uuid "984ca6d5-aa97-5d84-8ae3-06c8f43e8370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463485} :answer-field/id #uuid "5285b4f0-a29a-5753-9eed-ad304e29d4c6" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3250d354-20d6-54d0-97fb-d9ecc4f3e804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463486} {:answer/id #uuid "e358fd30-0443-5b9f-be75-140d1a5f4544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463487} :answer-field/id #uuid "c43a9049-f742-5536-9ca5-222ebee9078f" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91c88aa7-9c4e-5dd7-9c30-608f94c03554" :question/math-academy-id "q-125507" :question/problem "![](images/86/866a1812bd31e0443becf35a7cc2b8df1bc84a49091a94250b60fe3533e4b172.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4232577b0687d781f702a196ae2c0060d3264c1f44a45a404a0f9da03bc0dd1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7cfcef37ed6a52d5bd374fae2d7a00c4d8012e9ea8dbef43165c10bcc9471be.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b012af-7da3-57e7-b3fe-ad695b25321d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257210} {:answer/id #uuid "394ffebc-fc15-59d2-b922-cf67e8668a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{9}+\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257288} {:answer/id #uuid "98f26b34-1c20-50e4-ae9f-0696b345d0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258130} {:answer/id #uuid "dee11791-9fa1-5f13-86b4-6751c7ac156e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}+\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258723} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7bf65f-10a0-5fb7-85b0-318f615112d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{9}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257210} :answer-field/id #uuid "4626c1aa-c3c9-5543-a9d9-8d0984251445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d57f8c60-94ea-5515-8561-35af7ac97700" :question/math-academy-id "q-12556" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236b60cf-ff8d-5674-870b-bcaaf8d37985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{5x}}{5}+\\frac{3x{e}^{5x}}{25}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257078} {:answer/id #uuid "4d557db5-ba1a-5b05-b356-74912875cd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}+\\frac{3x{e}^{-5x}}{25}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257446} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1550eb-3fe9-5df7-a495-0e900c02c6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{25}-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257606} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffe0799-f21d-5fea-a233-c69798ab78fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5x}}{25}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257623} {:answer/id #uuid "92ec0b8f-683f-538a-921f-014ccb202f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x{e}^{-5x}}{25}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5x}}{25}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257623} :answer-field/id #uuid "793c5074-4fab-55cd-94b8-d8144c552545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a269e9e4-ba48-5393-8b91-051e5b1bc073" :question/math-academy-id "q-12559" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x+5\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-5x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x+5)\\cdot {e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x+5)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5x}}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109d035c-6ffc-56c3-8581-53bca2233532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256935} {:answer/id #uuid "209845fa-bfdf-5169-8a21-91e5d6fa04ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{2x+5}}{4}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257046} {:answer/id #uuid "5be69881-f41a-506d-a346-ccfa892bacb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{4}-\\frac{x{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257582} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4a072e-05a9-58e4-83e0-911e978158f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257721} {:answer/id #uuid "a0da683e-5345-5b81-9094-abd5ff0ec151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257721} :answer-field/id #uuid "b284ab05-c542-58e4-99b1-8ce31da489d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b258b659-45f7-5842-b37a-ee530a2eb1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-12560" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+7\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+7)\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+7)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9c996c-5a73-566e-bbb7-6b0823547575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463530} {:answer/id #uuid "30a2656e-d4ea-5f17-9193-df6e7c38a132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463530} :answer-field/id #uuid "d493a8c4-7d2e-51cc-ac4a-a63d54af6e3d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464699} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4919df2b-1781-51fe-810e-b5fcd6350e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463532} {:answer/id #uuid "ae069756-d16c-5e63-af48-f69f18b462c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463533} :answer-field/id #uuid "d91f3df4-dbea-5df3-a3ae-7785cf6bf841" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98296909-a163-589b-af22-9510086818fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463534} {:answer/id #uuid "5c79980a-5b04-5a68-ac76-67ef430a4d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463535} :answer-field/id #uuid "592f6dad-8033-5ef7-8c01-64650a21f312" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dc38a3d9-696c-596f-b353-c55f7e71f2d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125688" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9dd2fb4478ac443020e9cbebd64a2c9e4cdf5c7b6d5ce5cede854e0d7a3d804.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/51/51fafa71ff31592d9692348087cf0a20f6bbaa3b05e8519b4109d44207899a39.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/22/2287c33ff81973bcaef70917452c97282e0d0dd9ef06141d5beee8b53dec5836.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d82670-955d-5fbd-bc9e-ff9b5595ca19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463536} {:answer/id #uuid "70953006-86df-56ba-885e-a59756ac68a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463536} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bbc6ae6-a433-51b7-9bcc-4234a55196ba" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3413fb0-066c-5f24-a376-7d72fef132fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463538} {:answer/id #uuid "090b215e-b754-554a-8109-db90d4abfb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463539} :answer-field/id #uuid "79b6f686-1017-5c39-b085-1d1f73f3ac2e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3c62597-d021-5cbd-8c81-864877afd3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463540} {:answer/id #uuid "9d291d8d-f31c-5932-8efc-6cd6f690f503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463540} :answer-field/id #uuid "162bbeb9-8561-5809-b4fd-17fcbbfe7d72" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e9d575c8-adec-5386-bb2f-10bc638decac" :question/math-academy-id "q-125706" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a423ab56220e438fb849d2025ab53b99bb74ef598e16110c911d691680130527.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works  against the particle for $x<0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x>0.$ \n\n![](images/cd/cd52e91c72d481980c9a48575da7ca3eaebd2ed27f96b1054cf5c1c5da1aa3d9.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a1/a1e8fa5262fc787b691b3753fc57ab39aceee7c57dbbe166c6da6f6effaf42dc.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3c9663-08c1-5564-8b3e-9a4cb24a1b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260291} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0039d1-87b3-5421-9835-596f77290d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260519} {:answer/id #uuid "78be4970-1a3c-5e99-9122-1218d037e460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260934} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3b733e-e65e-576d-907c-703a87474361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261682} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fe109f-e706-559b-8fbc-078129db3af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260519} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9230d4f-f744-5083-8213-e8546aee6c18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3531e29b-2af9-5fd2-b0f4-3424f365a4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-126498" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=5$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eaebfb6-259d-5705-8c51-8711af08443f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260137} {:answer/id #uuid "83d5b435-ad62-5aba-8858-af5a85b263b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261020} {:answer/id #uuid "8de52fa9-07d3-55ac-b5db-21acd7458485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261117} {:answer/id #uuid "c33bd9d9-4ed1-58ae-9644-70021d5a13a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261599} {:answer/id #uuid "e574ef85-82f0-5cd8-ada8-d11da8587242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261117} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c4cd54-301a-5ffb-9364-f72617508b78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8db4a866-2f87-543f-a3e4-3db1b1a6a407" :question/math-academy-id "q-126502" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨6,-4,1⟩$ and $q=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+(-4)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18620d11-ce60-57c2-a580-d44a5579200c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260090} {:answer/id #uuid "3218e783-1e46-5bd3-bd29-5548bb105ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260326} {:answer/id #uuid "76437869-a2cd-55ab-8ebf-6ce70a057af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260908} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4d8c81-c3e3-5854-a2b5-53f7aae6f342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261523} {:answer/id #uuid "e35d583f-1e94-5ce0-ace0-3d795dc7095a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260090} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cb0a17-877b-5728-bbcf-86808ed0219e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ae07821-3e5f-5e7b-b438-d7c5726b59a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126890" :question/problem "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044e4eb6-1300-50e2-a121-217e4f2aeedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463598} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae2d382-db37-5f46-820f-5c44d57833a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463599} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff3851d-179e-55d1-8e85-49918b1df7d6" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1fc3250-01cb-5753-a77b-5c0da4ff1229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463600} {:answer/id #uuid "97324fd8-5eb3-50f1-81e7-c6ece2d89e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463600} :answer-field/id #uuid "39af33cf-4ee9-5a22-a356-af409e61b504" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e460cb-9bf3-5530-b17c-fa21d39cd66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463602} {:answer/id #uuid "9885935d-12a7-5807-9f71-24698900d591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4266908-76f9-5cdc-9e19-e4eb00cbd6de" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "18437e48-5bf8-597d-848c-dabff126da0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127025" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af413c3-686c-5c2d-bd12-502defdc9a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463604} {:answer/id #uuid "278d4075-be3f-56a0-8ec4-3e7094bec024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463604} :answer-field/id #uuid "2137d776-bbde-5eeb-966a-9a2b6a799b38" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9512c2ad-6323-5b3d-99cc-416c3e5fc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463606} {:answer/id #uuid "82f0e2e4-094c-54e4-8fe5-dcd89dd04ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463606} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d19a22-2c6f-5c38-a191-01fc2a500890" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e64c3d63-ef55-5f3e-bd1f-c776629cc749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463608} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89e298-102b-5364-846f-ceb612b0dd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463609} :answer-field/id #uuid "441f3c55-cced-5818-b31e-226165dab266" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e84dd576-6f5a-58c5-bf96-4c1a434830ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-127029" :question/problem "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5323d7-d924-5036-a404-e42edaf50898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463610} {:answer/id #uuid "8215cbff-cc90-5f37-8a5a-22a2f081bfef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463611} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff70dd5-877c-59ef-81bc-8178f3f49dec" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d6f8d00-d75f-502f-bc54-6f1ccf5e06f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463612} {:answer/id #uuid "aac8e4b0-3d27-5f75-bde9-f37d964b4094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463613} :answer-field/id #uuid "82e64186-1575-540c-b2e9-ea65c962ca59" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fa00f6-11fc-5e06-be3e-f6dba2027667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463614} {:answer/id #uuid "10df1e34-71fc-5eaf-b9c8-3da308db6b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8632d559-2742-5101-9099-c7c7ae2c0458" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92477238-fcf4-57c0-a788-d4cbd2b1a3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127030" :question/problem "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596e2f25-6344-5eef-8a75-204c85175502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463616} {:answer/id #uuid "f2be65cc-9a49-5f0b-8eef-59b759de229f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463616} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7d9b2de-be53-5786-80d4-7cae45fb50a3" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "497c22f7-4edc-50c1-acb2-19f74b2baca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463618} {:answer/id #uuid "e235a714-1f0d-523a-8c12-6f930cfc72c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463619} :answer-field/id #uuid "45cc04e2-3594-5873-8ddb-dade30e36c6f" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce71fa89-a63c-5932-a85a-15e6f162c9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463620} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffa6d1d-e27b-5ef6-bf18-d9dcd01130db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463621} :answer-field/id #uuid "78427e4a-7c4c-55d1-bdbd-e9b61519ab8b" :answer-field/key "The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6542e3fc-27ed-5a91-abb0-bbb5f848f669" :question/math-academy-id "q-127032" :question/problem "![](images/45/453633f6309b9f1d2a3f7a8d4266ff10f571823cffd705d0bc93b930dce63d8f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/84/844d5e5574571ae221a86f541820a4b215766451031f4eea678cee46a1b83e41.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b31d46646db2d88d8a3f5658ee876383795ad5ba9aa55eb9959d5f95c02dbe.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aced5e1-8641-5fed-a199-e5810c31368a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260252} {:answer/id #uuid "36d6919c-ff3a-5148-ab21-32b03a24ba12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260366} {:answer/id #uuid "902a1d36-1807-54ae-be76-2c49e245e66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261128} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d705d7-3e90-5c01-b0ee-80cf3f83501a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261303} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e25d3a-5b47-51f9-a030-43d19298c404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261303} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e22ec6-b427-52ff-a35f-b4cc82a25268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a35f06e-a951-5ad4-b83c-3bd955e95960" :question/math-academy-id "q-127049" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064ac3bd-68bf-5d7e-96ef-f16e2f295add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259931} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb4f557-d11e-580e-bee0-d908cc4fd0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260543} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4ba798-036b-5fb5-b057-c5ec468f5bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260676} {:answer/id #uuid "747cb36c-e574-588b-b6da-8d1419ba5e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260894} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7be33-e0d4-5074-ac91-c596c897aa80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261980} :answer-field/id #uuid "055a7271-4a54-504f-8c0f-5f25db56cbdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd38dff4-6be5-5901-81f2-c7b236232887" :question/math-academy-id "q-127050" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f8af75aece2346ffbf7a09e1ee90b6e0270f5661dd74e068abb2b2a848e5b97.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a83c3476bbe2ed2872ed8938dda59c5d0e6d58d05ecaf662748e7a184a4994a4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}({x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ff58a9-31d9-52b6-9539-5a96afef5cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259913} {:answer/id #uuid "87cf9463-02d9-5650-9a7a-bd75cf50def2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261058} {:answer/id #uuid "98e4b0c5-5690-5070-9b90-1bf0473522bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261200} {:answer/id #uuid "a27a5529-cca2-57ba-871d-ed34369afc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261291} {:answer/id #uuid "c4317848-9295-5ac7-92a2-f763f5f1951c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261291} :answer-field/id #uuid "27741482-00f8-5769-86bf-3cde1eeab0b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e37a59b-ba01-56a0-982c-60d7797b3b3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127053" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-i.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (-i)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "299aaae1-8475-55bf-8b13-35a76234f367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy+{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260241} {:answer/id #uuid "4221d1d5-a811-5dd0-9dcb-746ffd0e2961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260453} {:answer/id #uuid "7493e335-b4cb-504c-9d89-5d43ba48df15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260895} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6b2b2c-adf6-5af1-ac56-fcf71743fa7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy+3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261100} {:answer/id #uuid "fa50487d-228a-5944-9440-c96410e564fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260453} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafc80d4-38a2-5de1-ac74-dfe54c6268a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a3880ec-3713-5f30-bb66-5de9c6716caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-127056" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3d68329d81150c4a975bae4963d4e480cefaef0f2a0a29a0648c3793e9fb462.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $4{x}^{2}y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137ef58b-7aa2-56ab-acf1-569d978ae99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260048} {:answer/id #uuid "47760d48-4b08-58db-9ae0-5ef94c117ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260496} {:answer/id #uuid "759ce143-e5ea-5feb-a66f-7d6fef516f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260905} {:answer/id #uuid "adc6fab4-f58a-5a62-ae5e-15a4b5933803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261403} {:answer/id #uuid "c1221186-e4d1-5209-8c59-aae7eb1e9afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260496} :answer-field/id #uuid "9252b2c4-87e1-538b-b027-ba338b40221c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0d38139-02d0-5e66-aef1-2d1bcd0dc8e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127057" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$"}]}
